AB  - Deception detection from behavioral landmark trajectories remains challenging because behavioral cues unfold across different temporal scales and may vary across cultural and interview contexts. We propose MSTGNet, a multi-scale temporal graph network that represents each modality as a learned temporal graph within 50-frame windows and applies local-to-meso multi-scale convolution to capture short behavioral variations. Video-level predictions are obtained by deterministic averaging of overlapping window outputs rather than by explicit long-context modeling. Evaluated under leakage-aware video-level stratified cross-validation with validationonly threshold selection using Youden's J statistic on an Indonesian dataset (I3D: 1568 videos) and a Westerncontext dataset (RLT: 121 videos), MSTGNet achieved 78.2% accuracy (AUC 0.835, MCC 0.566) on I3D and the highest threshold-free performance on RLT (AUC 0.799). Multi-seed Wilcoxon tests showed that the AUC improvement over a comparable-capacity Transformer baseline remained significant on both datasets after Holm-Bonferroni correction (p < 0.001). Gradient attribution produced model-level saliency patterns consistent with modality ablation results, but these patterns should not be interpreted as causal behavioral evidence. Crossdataset transfer remained limited, suggesting domain shift beyond threshold miscalibration. The I3D dataset is publicly released to support reproducible cross-cultural research in video-based deception detection.
