TI  - Graph neural networks for seismic vulnerability assessment and retrofit prioritization of transportation networks
T2  - RELIABILITY ENGINEERING & SYSTEM SAFETY
LA  - English
KW  - Graph neural networks
KW  - Seismic vulnerability
KW  - Transportation networks
KW  - Fragility analysis
KW  - Critical infrastructure protection
KW  - Uncertainty quantification
KW  - RESILIENCE
KW  - INFRASTRUCTURE
KW  - SYSTEMS
AB  - Urban mobility and emergency access depend on transportation networks that must retain functional reliability after earthquakes. This study develops a physics-supervised, dual-branch Graph Neural Network (GNN) framework, termed DualGINE-HGT, that fuses network topology, geometric features, and probabilistic ground-motion inputs to predict calibrated link-level failure probabilities (P) over cap (cal)(f) for seismic road-network vulnerability assessment. The framework combines a Line-Graph Isomorphism Network with Edge features (GINE) capturing segment-to-segment topology with a Heterogeneous Graph Transformer (HGT) capturing segment-intersection geometric interactions, and is trained end-to-end against physics-based lognormal fragility labels ; the monotone structure of the fragility-derived supervision produces emergent physical plausibility without requiring an explicit penalty term. Robustness is established through spatial five-fold K-means cross-validation (Adjusted Rand Index = 0.9987 across ten clustering seeds), a five-seed deep ensemble for epistemic uncertainty, and per-period temperature scaling for probability calibration. On the Seaside (Oregon) coastal testbed (585 segments, 438 intersections, six seismic return periods, 3510 observations), the ensemble achieves Mean Absolute Error (MAE) = 0.0158, coefficient of determination (R-2) = 0.9777, Expected Calibration Error (ECE) = 0.0049, and a Monotonicity Violation Rate (MVR) of 0.24%; it outperforms XGBoost and LightGBM baselines by 22.3% and 27.1% in MAE respectively, with large effect sizes (Cohen's d = -1.35 and -1.50; paired t-test p = 0.039 and 0.028). Architecture ablations show the dual-branch design outperforms single-architecture GNNs (Graph Attention Network, GAT; GraphSAGE; Graph Convolutional Network, GCN) by 12-13% in MAE, and Physics-Informed Neural Network (PINN) baselines built on GCN and GAT backbones (PINN-GCN, PINN-GAT) degrade MAE by 83-88% relative to DualGINE-HGT, motivating the fragility-supervision design. Interpretability is delivered through SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analyses on partial-fidelity surrogates, triangulated with permutation importance. A system-level connectivity reliability analysis reveals nonlinear network degradation and critical fragmentation thresholds across seismic scenarios. Coupled with a multi-criteria consequence index , validated through four alternative weighting schemes and a 400-sample Monte Carlo stress test (Spearman rho = 0.982), the framework produces risk-informed retrofit priorities for coastal transportation lifelines.
