TI  - ToxiGenFormer-StructToxiNet synergy: advancing gender-specific toxicity profiling and molecular substructure insights
T2  - EUROPEAN PHYSICAL JOURNAL PLUS
LA  - English
AB  - Accurate prediction of drug toxicity is essential for personalized drug safety evaluation, regulatory decision-making, and rational compound design. However, many computational approaches insufficiently capture demographic variability, toxicity-driving substructures, and complex interactions within heterogeneous biological data. To address these challenges, we propose ToxiGenFormer-StructToxiNet Synergy (TGFS-Net), a unified deep learning framework that integrates heterogeneous multimodal toxicological data from the FAERS, Open TG-GATEs, and Tox21 datasets. TGFS-Net leverages complementary molecular and contextual information derived from SMILES sequences and molecular graphs to comprehensively characterize chemical compounds. A Transformer-based SMILES encoder (ToxiGenFormer) and a graph neural network with Substructure-Aware Attention Pooling (StructToxiNet) are trained simultaneously to learn both global semantics of chemicals and toxicity-related structural motifs. A Hierarchical Feature Fusion (HFF) module is used to combine modality-specific embeddings with co-attention and gated interactions to describe cross-modal interactions. GSCL expressly organizes the latent representations in gendered responses to toxicity to facilitate demographic vigor. The optimization of the model is done through AdamW with uncertainty-aware dynamic weighting of tasks, and robustness is measured by five-fold gender-stratified cross-validation. Interpretability is obtained using Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) and a Counterfactual Substructure Generator (CSG) to discover molecular components that augment or reduce toxicity. TGFS-Net presents good performance with an AUC score of 0.950 on FAERS, 0.943 on Open TG-GATEs and 0.939 on Tox21, better than the established baselines. The framework also scores high on accuracy in gender classification (94.6), age-group prediction with a high predictive performance of more than 91. Although uncommon toxicity subtypes are yet to be clinically tackled, TGFS-Net has offered a strong, interpretable and demographically conscious precision toxicology basis.
